Now I see the full picture of color handling. In `setup_game`, colors are derived from the mana cost:

```rust
// Derive colors from mana cost.
let colors: Vec<Color> = card_data.cost.as_ref()
    .map(|cost| {
        let mut c = Vec::new();
        for sym in &cost.symbols {
            ...
        }
        c
    })
    .unwrap_or_default();
```

So colors ARE derived from mana cost and stored on the object when the game is set up. This is how regular card objects in the game have their colors set.


But when creating a token copy, the colors are hardcoded to an empty vector with a TODO comment, which means tokens lose their color information entirely. So if Cackling Counterpart copies a white creature like Chapel Geist, the resulting token would have no colors instead of inheriting the original's colors.

This affects color-based checks in two places: targeting filters like `TargetFilter::Nonblack` and combat logic that checks if an attacker and blocker share a color. Let me look into what that combat check is actually doing.

